<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Gallaecia</h1><p class="st-meta">293 CE – 409 CE (approx.) · capital Bracara Augusta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The late Roman province of north-western Iberia, created by Diocletian and soon to become the home of the Suebic kingdom.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Gallaecia was formed around 293 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian's</a> reform, which split <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis</a> into smaller provinces. It covered the lands of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/callaeci">Callaeci</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/astures">Astures</a>, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-biscay">Bay of Biscay</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Bracara Augusta</a> (Braga) as its capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A governor ran the province under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/diocese-of-hispania">Diocese of Hispania</a>. Its economy was rural, based on farming, herding and the remains of the gold-mining industry around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturica-augusta">Asturica Augusta</a>. Towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucus-augusti">Lucus Augusti</a> (Lugo), with its surviving walls, were fortified in the late 3rd century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> spread, and the ascetic movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/priscillian">Priscillian</a>, executed in 385, found strong support here.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 409 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/suebi">Suebi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Hasding Vandals</a> entered Gallaecia, and the Suebi made it the centre of their kingdom. Roman rule faded, as recorded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hydatius">Hydatius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquae-flaviae">Aquae Flaviae</a>, and the name lived on in the medieval <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia">Kingdom of Galicia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gallaecia.jpg" alt="Gallaecia" width="320" height="294"><figcaption>Image: Alexandre Vigo · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gallaecia.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>293 CE – 409 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bracara Augusta</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>, <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Callaecia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=351&amp;focus=gallaecia" aria-label="Gallaecia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4475 697 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gallaecia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4795 953L5304 942M4795 953L5031 967M4795 953L4795 953"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5031 967h0M4795 953h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4795 953h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4795 953h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bracara Augusta. <a href="/atlas?year=351&amp;focus=gallaecia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
